    "section_id": "s0003",
    "source_line_ids": [
      "p0001-n0010-l0000"
    ],
    "source_pages": [
      1
    ],
    "text": "Summary"
  },
  {
    "block_id": "b000012",
    "issues": [],
    "kind": "prose",
    "repairs": [
      {
        "left": "p0001-n0011-l0000",
        "operation": "line_wrap_to_space",
        "right": "p0001-n0011-l0001"
      },
      {
        "left": "p0001-n0011-l0001",
        "operation": "line_wrap_to_space",
        "right": "p0001-n0011-l0002"
      },
      {
        "left": "p0001-n0011-l0002",
        "operation": "line_wrap_to_space",
        "right": "p0001-n0011-l0003"
      }
    ],
    "section_id": "s0003",
    "source_line_ids": [
      "p0001-n0011-l0000",
      "p0001-n0011-l0001",
      "p0001-n0011-l0002",
      "p0001-n0011-l0003"
    ],
    "source_pages": [
      1
    ],
    "text": "During the revolution of music, interactions within and between genres can have a critical impact on the direction of musical change. In this paper, we develop models to analyze the in\ufb02uence between musicians, measure the degree of similarity between music, and reveal the changes in genres over time, as well as the in\ufb02uence of social, cultural, and historical aspects."
  },
  {
    "block_id": "b000013",
    "issues": [
      "hyphen_join_review"
    ],
    "kind": "prose",
    "repairs": [
      {
        "left": "p0001-n0012-l0000",
        "operation": "line_wrap_to_space",
        "right": "p0001-n0012-l0001"
      },
      {
        "left": "p0001-n0012-l0001",
        "operation": "line_wrap_to_space",
        "right": "p0001-n0012-l0002"
      },
      {
        "left": "p0001-n0012-l0002",
        "operation": "line_wrap_to_space",
        "right": "p0001-n0012-l0003"
      },
      {
        "left": "p0001-n0012-l0003",
        "operation": "line_wrap_to_space",
        "right": "p0001-n0012-l0004"
      },
      {
        "left": "p0001-n0012-l0004",
        "operation": "line_wrap_to_space",
        "right": "p0001-n0012-l0005"
      },
      {
        "left": "p0001-n0012-l0005",
        "operation": "line_wrap_to_space",
        "right": "p0001-n0012-l0006"
      }
    ],
    "section_id": "s0003",
    "source_line_ids": [
      "p0001-n0012-l0000",
      "p0001-n0012-l0001",
      "p0001-n0012-l0002",
      "p0001-n0012-l0003",
      "p0001-n0012-l0004",
      "p0001-n0012-l0005",
      "p0001-n0012-l0006"
    ],
    "source_pages": [
      1
    ],
    "text": "In TASK 1: First, we take each musician as a node and calculate the directed in\ufb02uence be- tween musicians as weights by considering the factors of time span and genre span to build a directed music in\ufb02uence network. Then we use social network analysis (SNA) to ana- lyze the in\ufb02uence network. The Degree Centrality of musicians in the network is calculated, and the Pagerank-modi\ufb01ed eigenvector centrality is further used to measure the in\ufb02uence of musicians(MI), obtaining that Bob Dylan, The Rolling Stones, Chuck Berry, and Elvis Presley have the highest in\ufb02uence. We also \ufb01nd that MI obeys power-law distribution, implying that"
  },
  {
    "block_id": "b000014",
    "issues": [],
    "kind": "title",
    "repairs": [],
    "section_id": "s0003",
    "source_line_ids": [
      "p0001-n0003-l0000"
    ],
    "source_pages": [
      1
    ],
    "text": "\u5fae\u4fe1\u53f7\uff1aMATHmodels"
  },
  {
    "block_id": "b000015",
    "issues": [],
    "kind": "prose",
    "repairs": [
      {
        "left": "p0001-n0012-l0007",
        "operation": "line_wrap_to_space",
        "right": "p0001-n0012-l0008"
      }
    ],
    "section_id": "s0003",
    "source_line_ids": [
      "p0001-n0012-l0007",
      "p0001-n0012-l0008"
    ],
    "source_pages": [
      1
    ],
    "text": "the in\ufb02uence network between in\ufb02uencers and followers is a scale-free network. Finally, we explore the sub-network to analyze the meaning of the subset of MI."
  },
  {
    "block_id": "b000016",
    "issues": [],
    "kind": "prose",
    "repairs": [
      {
        "left": "p0001-n0013-l0000",
